:"native-latex","boundaryCheck":"all printed question markers match native sequence"}},{"id":"practice/maths-a-level/maths-mechanics-year-1/ch8/8.4/q1","courseId":"maths-a-level","bookId":"maths-mechanics-year-1","chapterId":"ch8","chapterNumber":8,"questionLabel":"1","chapterTitle":"Modelling in mechanics","subchapterId":"8.4","subchapterTitle":"8.4 Working with vectors","number":"1","question":"Julia drives from her home along a straight road to work at a constant speed of 42 mph.\nOn her way home from work she stops at her daughter&#x27;s school to pick her up.\nShe drives from work to the school at a constant speed of 28 mph.\nShe drives from the school to her home at a constant speed of 34 mph.\nJulia&#x27;s home, work and the school all lie on a straight line, as shown in the diagram.\n<img style=\"max-width:65mm\" class=\"practice-figure\"  src=\"content/practice/assets/dfa6f88958f17f27eff3.png\" alt=\"Original question diagram\">\nTaking the positive direction as shown in the diagram, state Julia&#x27;s:\n<ol type=\"a\"><li value=\"1\">velocity on the journey from her home to work</li><li value=\"2\">displacement from her home when she reaches work</li><li value=\"3\">velocity on the journey from work to the school</li><li value=\"4\">displacement from work when she reaches the school</li><li value=\"5\">velocity on the journey from the school to her home.</li></ol>","hints":["You need to choose a positive direction when considering motion in a straight line. When an object is moving in the opposite direction to the positive direction its velocity is negative."],"steps":[],"solutionStatus":"pending","skillIds":[],"topicIds":[],"source":{"kind":"practice-booklet","pdf":"resources/practice/applied-year-1/chapter-08.pdf","pdfSha256":"9d8ef6696b7c62a15bf8354eae27ac596ce9a69fc6ac6a2bec4036e918148b84","nativeSha256":"38d6c6a4a898c01cef255fecfd79e5b385c5fbba288c8aea8ce328cda90450b8","questionLatexSha256":"b1e1fcb1cbb905103225baa027b8ce1350c63eb8e566ca6e1fe758455917